3. So b=3.\n\nNow combine with c1: for a=0, b ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\nThus, for a=0:\n\n- c=1, b=0: b=0 allowed. So tuple (0,0,1,0) valid.\n- c=1, b=2: b=2 not allowed (c1 only allows b=0,1,3). So invalid.\n- c=3, b=3: b=3 allowed. So tuple (0,3,3,0) valid.\n\nThus, only two tuples for a=0.\n\nCase a=1:\n\nc1: b ∈ {3,2}\nc4: for a=1, allowed (d,a) pairs: (0,1), (1,1), (2,1). So d ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nNow c3: allowed (c,d) pairs: (1,0), (3,1), (1,1), (3,0), (2,2). So for each d:\n\n- d=0: allowed c ∈ {1 (pair (1,0)), 3 (pair (3,0))}. So c ∈ {1,3}.\n- d=1: allowed c ∈ {1 (pair (1,1)), 3 (pair (3,1))}. So c ∈ {1,3}.\n- d=2: allowed c = 2 (pair (2,2)). So c=2.\n\nNow c2: allowed (b,c) pairs: (0,1), (0,0), (3,3), (1,0), (2,1). So for each c:\n\n- c=1: allowed b ∈ {0 (pair (0,1)), 2 (pair (2,1))}. So b ∈ {0,2}.\n- c=3: allowed b = 3 (pair (3,3)). So b=3.\n- c=2: allowed b? No pair with c=2. So c=2 is invalid.\n\nThus, c cannot be 2 because then c2 fails. So we must exclude d=2 case.\n\nThus, for a=1, d ∈ {0,1}.\n\nNow for each d and c:\n\n- d=0, c=1: b ∈ {0,2}. But c1 for a=1 allows b ∈ {3,2}. Intersection: b=2 only (since b=0 not allowed). So b=2.\n\n- d=0, c=3: b must be 3 (c2). Intersection with c1: b=3 allowed. So b=3.\n\n- d=1, c=1: b ∈ {0,2}. Intersection with c1: b=2 only. So b=2.\n\n- d=1, c=3: b=3 allowed. So b=3.\n\nThus, four tuples:\n\n- (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=0)\n- (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=0)\n- (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=1)\n- (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=1)\n\nThus, total six left tuples.\n\nThus, our enumeration is correct.\n\nNow we need to check cross constraints for each left tuple. We already checked L1 (0,0,1,0) works. L2 (0,3,3,0) fails because c9 fails. L3 (1,2,1,0) we need to check. L4 (1,3,3,0). L5 (1,2,1,1). L6 (1,3,3,1). We need to check if any of those also fail.\n\nWe already checked L1 works. Let's check L3: (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=0).\n\nConstraints:\n\n- b=2, c9 (b,e) allowed pairs: (0,2) and (1,3). b=2 is not allowed, so no allowed e. So L3 fails as well. Indeed, c9 only allows b=0 or b=1. So any left tuple with b=2 or b=3 will fail c9. So all left tuples with b=2 or b=3 are invalid. That includes L2 (b=3), L3 (b=2), L4 (b=3), L5 (b=2), L6 (b=3). So only L1 (b=0) is possible. So the left component enumeration includes only one tuple that can be extended (L1). All other five are invalid due to c9.\n\nThus, the left component tuple enumeration is underdetermined (i.e., many left tuples cannot be extended). So assessment \"underdetermined\" is correct.\n\nThus, the candidate tuples should be the enumeration of all satisfying left component tuples (the six we found). That is what we have.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the envelope includes candidate_tuples as an array of arrays. Yes.\n\nNow we need to ensure that we didn't include any extra fields like \"candidate_tuples\" that might be considered not part of the template. But the role says we need to edit candidate tuples, so we must include it. The envelope template didn't have it, but we need to add it. The user said \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" This could be interpreted as we need to output exactly the envelope as per the template, but we can edit the fields. The template includes \"payload.report.target\". It doesn't include \"candidate_tuples\". However, the role says we need to edit candidate tuples. So maybe the envelope template is incomplete and we need to add it. The user might have intentionally omitted candidate_tuples to test if we follow the role. The role says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we must edit candidate tuples. So we must include candidate_tuples.\n\nThus, we will output the JSON with candidate_tuples.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the JSON is valid and no extra whitespace or newlines before the JSON. The output should be raw JSON. So we will output the JSON as a single line? It can be formatted with newlines and indentation, but it's still valid JSON. The instruction says \"Output raw unformatt